Parmenides: being, thought, and the force of an argument (@greek-parmenides: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @greek-nature-being, #source @greek-source-fairbanks, #source @greek-source-burnet, #philosophy @greek-eleatic-arguments, #ancient-greek-philosophy @greek-eleatic-arguments, #philosophy @greek-atomists, #ancient-greek-philosophy @greek-atomists) { r_7HVRAJY7AF3178NEH1N8BF9NPV
**Read:** [Fairbanks' The First Philosophers of Greece](../sources/Presocratics-Fairbanks.lingua#r_0CETPRSWH0WZT8335D8692RNJY), Chapter VI, “Parmenides”: the English translation of the poem's opening and the way of truth, then the treatment of the way of opinion and the ancient testimonies. Compare [Burnet's Early Greek Philosophy](../sources/Early-Greek-Philosophy-Burnet.lingua#r_1DSKX3DB6E343PZHZABGATVK13), Chapter IV, “Parmenides of Elea”, tracking which conclusions rest on the poem and which on later interpretation.

**Portuguese alternative:** [Os pré-socráticos: fragmentos, doxografia e comentários, Nova Cultural](../sources/Presocratics-Nova-Cultural.lingua#r_6ZA1ENV6PB59E6EGC7MCBS1782). Use the corresponding thinker's section, keeping fragments, ancient reports, and commentary distinct. The guide's question can be investigated in either collection; identify the edition and passage when comparing them.

**Preparation:** [premises, inference, and an objection](../foundations/Read-an-argument.lingua#r_4GMQCKPBF9BCSKDK0KPN013EBN). Introduce the distinction between a claim about reality and a claim about what can be thought or said as it appears in the passage. [Heraclitus](Heraclitus.lingua#r_5K10CZBC41P0MXC7M3M1NW8MKY) supplies a useful comparison concerning change.

Reconstruct the steps through which the poem reaches conclusions about what is. Do not substitute the conclusion for its argument. Ask whether a transition from what cannot be thought or spoken to what cannot exist requires an additional premise.

**Practice:** select a short chain of reasoning and number its premises. Indicate where the meanings of being, not-being, thinking, or saying matter. Offer an objection that targets one of those transitions, then consider how a defender might respond.

**Check:** explain why a claim that conflicts with ordinary experience still requires an examination of its reasoning. Identify whether your objection challenges the premise, the inference, or your interpretation of the text.

**Broaden:** [Zeno and Melissus on motion and plurality](Zeno-and-Melissus.lingua#r_5WR6CJM0JV5QPDX2P5177J0YT4).

**Next:** [The atomists](Atomists.lingua#r_666ZSW8DNDQ45TJKB8K9HKVS5B). **Crossing:** [arguments about soul and intelligible reality](../plato/Phaedo.lingua#r_6JFZZP5W43FY7FFQENQNH96AFQ). **Return:** [Nature and being](Nature-and-being.lingua#r_61M87EN28QQ9AHXT80SPCB0CVH) · [Ancient Greek philosophy](../Ancient-Greek-Philosophy.lingua#r_464ZHR7ZYC234A0XX2QEW47VB3).
